15:04:57 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s not quite right. IPv6 was designed **well before IPv4 addresses were exhausted**, specifically because engineers recognized early that IPv4’s 32-bit address space—about 4.3 billion addresses—would eventually be insufficient. A rough timeline: - **Early 1990s:** Internet engineers were already discussing IPv4 address depletion and routing-table growth. - **1994–1995:** The IETF selected what became IPv6 and published early specifications and transition plans. - **1998:** IPv6 was standardized in its first major form. - **2011:** IANA allocated its last large IPv4 address blocks to regional registries. - **2010s onward:** Regional registries progressively exhausted their available pools. So IPv6 was not invented as an emergency response after exhaustion. It was a planned successor developed roughly 15–20 years beforehand. What *did* catch the industry somewhat off guard was the **slow adoption** of IPv6. NAT, CIDR, address reclamation, and later IPv4 address markets extended IPv4’s useful life far longer than many forecasts expected. Those workarounds reduced the urgency to migrate, so by the time free IPv4 pools were largely gone, much of the Internet still depended heavily on IPv4.
