15:05:16 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That is a fair distinction: having an IPv6 specification and transition mechanisms was not the same as having an enforceable, Internet-wide deployment program. There *was* planning beyond the spec: - IPv6 was designed to coexist with IPv4 for a long transition period (“dual stack”). - Transition technologies were standardized: tunnels, translators, later NAT64/DNS64, and others. - IANA, regional registries, governments, operators, vendors, and standards bodies promoted or required IPv6 in various contexts. - Major operating systems, routers, mobile networks, and content providers eventually added IPv6 support. But there was no single organization able to require every ISP, enterprise, website operator, device vendor, and customer network to upgrade on a shared deadline. The Internet is decentralized, and for many operators IPv4 plus NAT continued to work adequately. Deploying IPv6 cost money, introduced operational risk, and often offered little immediate customer-visible benefit. So the failure was not that nobody anticipated IPv4 exhaustion. It was that the incentives and coordination mechanisms were too weak to achieve universal IPv6 deployment before free IPv4 allocations became scarce. Also, “we ran out” has a specific meaning: registries ran out of broadly available *new* IPv4 blocks. The Internet did not run out of functioning IPv4 connectivity, because existing allocations remained in use and NAT, address transfers, and sharing stretched the remaining address space. That bought time—but also reduced the pressure to finish the migration.
